 ## Subtopics
 
-### Scene Editing
-A technique to modify specific spatial regions of a generated 3D scene by treating the 3D coarse latent as an image and applying diffusion-based inpainting [S2]. Unlike global latent resampling which regenerates the entire scene, this method allows localized modifications by inpainting only a selected region of $c$ [S2]. It works by noising the region to be kept, concatenating it with the region being sampled, passing through the diffusion model, and applying reconstruction guidance to harmonize boundaries [S2]. After obtaining the updated coarse latent $c$, the fine latent is re-sampled conditioned on the new $c$ [S2].
+### [Scene Editing](17595-scene-editing.md)
+Scene editing modifies specific spatial regions of a generated 3D scene by treating the 3D coarse latent representation as an image and applying diffusion-based inpainting. Unlike global latent resampling which regenerates the entire scene, this method allows localized modifications by inpainting only a selected region of the coarse latent. It enables control over color, opacity, lighting magnitude, and light direction in inverse volume rendering contexts.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time and Memory Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
-*   **Measured Performance:** Not given in the sources.
-*   **Scaling:** Not given in the sources.
-*   **Strengths:** Leverages pre-trained conditional models for stable and efficient image synthesis based on text prompts or other conditional inputs [S1]. Hierarchical structure allows modeling complex dependencies between global geometry, coarse structure, and fine details [S2]. Scene editing allows localized modifications without regenerating the entire scene [S2].
-*   **Limitations:** Not given in the sources.
-*   **When to use:** When generating 3D scenes with hierarchical structure (global, coarse, fine) is required, or when localized editing of 3D scenes is needed [S2].
-*   **When not to use:** Not given in the sources.
+*   **Computational Efficiency:** By performing diffusion in a latent space, LDMs significantly improve computational efficiency compared to pixel-space diffusion [S1].
+*   **Training Stability:** The use of v-prediction parameterization enhances convergence and training stability [S2].
+*   **Parallel Training:** The hierarchical components ($\psi_g, \psi_c, \psi_f$) can be trained in parallel, reducing training time [S2].
+*   **Quality:** LDMs produce outputs with reasonable texture and geometry, though post-optimization (e.g., Score Distillation Sampling) may be required for high-fidelity results on real-world data [S2].
+*   **Limitations:** The quality of the generated voxels depends heavily on the pre-trained latent auto-encoder and the conditioning mechanisms. The hierarchical structure requires careful alignment of the latent spaces for $g, c,$ and $f$ [S2].
 
 ## Background
-Latent Diffusion Models (LDMs) perform diffusion in a latent space, significantly improving computational efficiency and output quality [S1]. Stable Diffusion further leverages pre-trained conditional models for stable and efficient image synthesis based on text prompts or other conditional inputs [S1]. The specific hierarchical LDM for 3D scene generation (NF-LDM) was introduced in "NeuralField-LDM: Scene Generation with Hierarchical Latent Diffusion Models" [S2]. The method builds on Denoising Diffusion Models (DDMs) [24] and uses v-prediction parameterization for better convergence and training stability [S2].
+Latent Diffusion Models were introduced to address the high computational cost of standard diffusion models in pixel space [S1]. Stable Diffusion, a prominent implementation of LDMs, leverages pre-trained conditional models for stable and efficient image synthesis based on text prompts or other conditional inputs [S1]. In the context of 3D volume rendering, hierarchical LDMs extend this concept to generate voxel-based neural fields by modeling the joint distribution of geometry, camera trajectories, and fine details [S2]. The method builds on Denoising Diffusion Probabilistic Models (DDPMs) [S2].
 
